a","attributes":{"href":"#the-jack-dorsey-effect-changed-the-scale"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["02"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["The Jack Dorsey effect changed the scale"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#pseudonyms-became-part-of-the-culture"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["03"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Pseudonyms became part of the culture"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#the-early-wave-was-tiny-enough-to-remember-names"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["04"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["The early wave was tiny enough to remember names"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#the-first-app-store-fight-became-community-memory"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["05"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["The first app-store fight became community memory"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#why-early-does-not-always-mean-authoritative"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["06"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Why early does not always mean authoritative"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#sources"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["07"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Sources worth opening"]}]},"\n            "]},"\n          "]},"\n          ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-content"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"header","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-header"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-meta"},"children":["\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["People"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["7 min read"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["Origins"]},"\n              "]},"\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{},"children":["The First Wave: Why Early Nostr People Still Matter"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Early users gave Nostr its strange first culture: Bitcoiners, open-source developers, protocol romantics, privacy people, public figures and ordinary testers willing to live inside unfinished software."]},"\n            "]},"\n\n            ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-callout"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Reader route:"]}," Read this for the first-wave context behind the current community: why early adopters still shape norms, language and trust.\n            "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"early-users-created-the-weather"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Early users created the weather"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Every social network has an early climate. Nostr's first climate was not polished consumer social. It was closer to a workshop with a public door: developers testing relays, Bitcoiners trying zaps, privacy people asking about keys, writers watching platform politics and users willing to tolerate broken clients because the idea felt important. That early weather still shapes the network. It explains the jokes, the suspicion of platforms, the obsession with key custody and the habit of talking directly to builders."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A People archive needs to preserve that period without turning it into mythology. Early users matter because they set norms. They also brought blind spots. A scene that begins with technical users can become hard for normal readers to enter. The archive has to show both the courage and the narrowness of the first wave."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"the-jack-dorsey-effect-changed-the-scale"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["The Jack Dorsey effect changed the scale"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Nostr was not born as a celebrity project, but Jack Dorsey's public attention and funding changed how outsiders looked at it. A small protocol suddenly had a famous witness to the failures of centralized social media. That did not make Nostr mature. It made Nostr visible. Visibility brought builders, opportunists, journalists, critics and users who wanted to see whether the promise was real."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["That moment matters because it changed the People layer. The community had to absorb mainstream curiosity while still behaving like an open-source scene. Some people wanted product polish. Some wanted ideological purity. Some wanted funding. Some wanted to keep hacking. The tension has never fully disappeared."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"pseudonyms-became-part-of-the-culture"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Pseudonyms became part of the culture"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Nostr's early culture made room for pseudonyms in a way mainstream professional networks often do not. A handle could be a builder, a relay operator, a critic, a meme account, a curator or a serious technical contributor. That created freedom for people who wanted public work without conventional exposure. It also created source problems. A pseudonym can be respected without being fully legible to a new reader."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["The People hub has to read pseudonyms through public work. What did the account build, write, host, fund, test or explain? Who references it? Which projects connect to it? Which claims remain uncertain? That is how the archive respects pseudonymity without becoming vague."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"the-early-wave-was-tiny-enough-to-remember-names"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["The early wave was tiny enough to remember names"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Early Nostr history has a strange intimacy. It is possible to talk about a handful of public names and actually cover much of the visible room: fiatjaf writing and arguing the protocol into existence, Jack Dorsey amplifying and funding it, William Casarin making Damus a public mobile moment, Vitor Pamplona building Amethyst into the Android power route, Hodlbod pushing Coracle and relay-aware thinking, and a wider Bitcoin media circle making the whole thing understandable to people who were not reading GitHub."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["That early wave matters because it set the culture before the market arrived. It normalized pseudonyms, zaps, public grants, small clients, blunt protocol arguments, rough edges and the expectation that users should own more of their social graph. Some of that culture is beautiful. Some of it can be hard for newcomers. A good archive should make both visible."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Early does not mean permanently right. It means historically useful."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"the-first-app-store-fight-became-community-memory"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["The first app-store fight became community memory"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Damus and the zap dispute became one of the early stories that people could repeat outside the Nostr bubble. It had a protagonist, a gatekeeper, a visible product and a simple conflict around money and speech. That kind of story is rare in protocol land. Most protocol stories are too abstract to travel. This one traveled because everyone understands an app store saying no."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["The lesson is larger than Apple. It showed the Nostr community what kind of pressure a successful app would face. Open protocols still meet closed distribution systems. Creator payments still meet platform rules. Builders still have to ship inside ecosystems they do not control. The early wave learned that freedom tech has to pass through very ordinary product gates."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["That is why the story belongs in a People route. It shaped how the community remembers itself."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"why-early-does-not-always-mean-authoritative"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Why early does not always mean authoritative"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Being early gives context, not permanent authority. Some early users became serious maintainers. Some became cultural anchors. Some left. Some repeated old answers after the network changed. A reader needs history but also present-tense evidence. The early wave is a starting chapter, not a rank system."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["That distinction keeps the archive honest. It can honor people who made the first room livable while still following newer builders, regional organizers, designers, wallet people and creators who are changing what Nostr feels like now."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n            ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section crays-article-sources","id":"sources"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Sources worth opening"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"ul","attributes":{},"children":[